reviewer_comments.reviewer_2:
Thank you for the opportunity to review your manuscript entitled “Artificial intelligence and the supply of critical minerals” to Nature Communications. The manuscript describes a novel economic theory that differentiates the types of risks associated with critical versus conventional raw materials. Overall, the manuscript is very well written and clearly describes the results and their implications. The topic of critical raw material supply constraints and artificial intelligence will likely be of broad interest. It was also great to see the analysis presented as an electronic supplementary table. My review is provided as an annotated PDF and a Word document. The annotated PDF highlights sentences that could be clarified and other minor comments. Below are more general comments for your consideration:
(1) The technical and non-technical risks of critical raw materials projects and how they differ from conventional raw materials need to be discussed in detail in the introduction and Section 2. The assumption that there are disparate risk profiles for different critical raw materials goes to the core of the manuscript’s thesis, but is not described. Readers are referred to the literature for finding this rationale on their own, but the most important references mentioned in the main body of the manuscript do not appear in the bibliography. A quick google scholar search for the author profiles did not return results for “Trench 2013” and “Trench and Packey 2014”. In the absence of any further information, it is not obvious to me why these different risk profiles are commodity specific, since geography and political risks have the potential to impact all commodities. Please list and describe the technical and non-technical risk factors and how they vary systemically for each critical raw material. This kind of background information is needed to support the assumptions underpinning the analysis;
(2) The papers also seems to assume that front- and back-end risks profiles are fixed for the conceptual section of the manuscript (Section 2). However, it is not obvious to me why these risks can’t be mitigated between discovery and development (or vice versa) to convert critical raw materials from back-ended to front-ended. Sections 3 and the text around equations 2 and 3 discuss the dynamic nature of these risk profiles within the context of AI, but I think some discussion on the other controlling factors for these risk profiles would help readers understand and interpret your results. Shortening the lead times of back-ended projects with AI seems to be distinct from the potential for AI, management, or policy interventions to be used, like a switch, to mitigate these risks all together. The probability that this could happen is higher for critical raw materials used in defence, where governments may directly intervene for national security considerations;
(3) The paper assumes that 50% of critical raw materials exhibit a back-ended risk premium, however not all critical minerals are of equal importance to the renewable energy transition. Moreover, different critical minerals are required in different volumes and have different monetary values. My understanding is that an average proportion (i.e., 50%) was selected based on the number of minerals that are either front- or back-ended with no further information provided. If my interpretation is correct, the assumption made in this manuscript seems too simplistic. The range of proportions (60-40%) for front- versus back-end risks addresses this caveat to some extent, but I think readers would benefit from some discussion on the relative importance of each critical raw material to the energy transition and how this average proportion was selected;
(4) I was also interested to learn that some critical minerals defined as front- or back-ended are in fact sourced from the same deposit type. For example, cobalt and nickel are both sourced, at least in part, from magmatic sulphide deposits. But cobalt is a back-end mineral; whereas nickel is a front-end mineral. The front- versus back-end risk paradigm and its fixture to commodities seems too simplistic for critical raw materials with multiple geological sources;
(5) I got confused between the types of government invested being proposed as part of the current study. As you probably know better than me, Government investments in critical raw materials is pretty complex. Tax incentives, pools of patient capital for financing, and nationalization of the entire minerals sector could also be considered “government investment”. I think the policy section would benefit from some clarity over how investing in AI differs from the current government approach. At the same time, it may be worth highlighting that investments in AI specific to critical raw materials may be distinct from AI investment made by governments more generally. Your analysis also demonstrates the most important stages to apply this AI investment to reduce back-end premium (i.e., all AI applications between exploration and development, including data-driven land-use planning, prospectivity modelling, geophysical-geochemical-remote sensing surveys, drilling sensors, 3D resource modelling, pre-competitive ESG). Adding more specificity over the types of AI investments and the role of government could increase the impact of your work;
(6) I am not an economist, but the idea that governments are the ones primarily responsible for reducing the back-ended risk premiums for the minerals sector seems controversial. Maybe this is a common assumption in this field of research. It is true that governments are already “investing” in different ways to de-risk mineral exploration. I work for a geological survey organization and so my job sort of depends on the idea that governments can support the discovery of critical raw materials. Investing in AI seems like a good idea to me because it is broad based and the rising tide of any technical breakthroughs could lift all boats. But surely the industry also has some role to invest in the type of AI that would shorten their own lead times and increase profits. Perhaps this is what you mean by “supranational organizations” and their role in reducing back-ended risk premiums. Please consider mentioning other industry-led initiatives that could reduce back-ended risk premiums in addition to government funding;
(7) I think the title could be improved to describe what this study actually shows. For example, something like “Artificial intelligence investments reduce risks to critical mineral supply” or “Securing critical mineral supply chains through investments in artificial intelligence” would give readers a much better sense for your main conclusions and would likely increase your impact. In my view, your work is far more interesting than the current title suggests.

Overall, this is a well written manuscript that will be of significant interest to a broad readership. The conceptual model and the analysis provides an economic perspective on the risks to the supply of critical raw materials. I am a geochemist by training and do not have enough expertise to place this study within the broader economics literature. The ideas and analysis seems novel to me, but other reviewers are needed to characterize the level of innovation within the context of economics. Nevertheless, I could follow the logic of the manuscript and can quite clearly see the significance of the work. I hope that my comments can help with your revision process. Thanks again for the opportunity to review your work. Congratulations and best wishes with your research.
Sincerely,
Chris
